  "Looking at the analysis:\n\n**The bug**: `response-entries` builds `by-key` including `:seon.repl/result` (from handle) and `:seon.repl/ms` (from duration), and those keys ARE in `response-order`. So `:result` and `:ms` are present in the map. But the test's rendered-value cases assert `:seon.repl/result` and `:seon.repl/ms` survive — and the note says a declared renderer's response drops them.\n\nLet me look at the path for rendered values. In `response-entries`, `value = (value-text emission)`, `rendered? = (some? (:seon.eval/renderer emission))`. The `:seon.repl/ms` is emitted only `when (and (int? duration) (not (:seon.repl/changed-since? emission)))`. And `:seon.repl/result` there — those are unconditional on `rendered?`. So why would they drop?\n\nLet me check `response-order` and whether `response-entries` is even what `response` uses — actually the source shows `response` calls `(reply-map (response-entries emission))`. Let me look at where the renderer path differs.\n\nLet me find `renderer-response` or where rendered values bypass.\n\n```clojure\n(filter #(re-find #\"response\" (name (key %))) (ns-publics 'seon.repl))\n```\n\nLet me instead just try the proof test now to see current failures — that's the ground truth I keep avoiding.\n\n```clojure\n(clojure.test/run-test-var #'seon.repl-test/the-reply-is-one-edn-map-whatever-the-value)\n```",
